    "kind": "lesson",
    "intro": "This lesson is about one thing: the workflow an investigator runs from the moment an alert lands to the moment the case is closed with a defensible root cause. It assumes you already read logs, know the common Windows and Entra ID event sources, and can write a basic query, so we skip all of that and spend the time on the method that separates a fast, correct investigation from a slow or wrong one.\n\nWe start at intake: claiming an alert, reading what actually matched, and reaching a true positive, expected activity or false positive verdict inside a timebox. Then come the five scoping questions (who, what, when, where, how) and a pivot cookbook organised by evidence source, with a query for each question across identity, endpoint, network, email and cloud. From there we build the timeline, including process-tree reconstruction, and learn to run competing hypotheses with explicit confidence levels instead of chasing the first story that fits.\n\nThe second half is about finishing the job. You will walk backwards to the initial access vector and patient zero, expand scope into identities, tokens, OAuth grants and mailbox rules, decide when to escalate, and weigh each containment action against what it destroys or reveals. Then we cover eradication, recovery and the criteria for closing, how to keep evidence and case notes that survive a handoff, the pitfalls that wreck investigations, and one complete case from alert to closure.\n\nQueries are written in KQL for Microsoft Defender XDR advanced hunting (Device*, Email*, UrlClickEvents, CloudAppEvents, AlertInfo and AlertEvidence) and Microsoft Sentinel (SigninLogs, AuditLogs, SecurityEvent). The workflow itself is product-neutral: the same pivots work in SPL or any other SIEM language once you swap the table and field names. Hostnames, accounts, domains and IPs are fictional.",
