        "heading": "Scoping Questions: Who, What, When, Where, How",
        "content": "A confirmed alert is one data point. Scoping turns it into a bounded incident by answering five questions, and every answer must point at evidence, not intuition. Write the five questions at the top of the case and fill them in as you go; the blanks are your to-do list.\n\n### The five questions and what answers them\n\n| Question | What you actually need | Primary evidence |\n|----------|------------------------|------------------|\n| **Who** | Every account involved: the one in the alert, accounts logged on to the same hosts, accounts the attacker created or used | DeviceLogonEvents, SecurityEvent 4624 / 4648 / 4672 / 4720, SigninLogs |\n| **What** | Every action taken: processes run, files written, persistence created, data touched | DeviceProcessEvents, DeviceFileEvents, DeviceRegistryEvents, CloudAppEvents |\n| **When** | The window from first malicious activity to now, in UTC | The earliest confirmed row in your timeline, plus retention limits |\n| **Where** | Every host, mailbox, tenant and cloud resource involved | Logon and network pivots, IOC sweeps, AuditLogs |\n| **How** | How they got in and how they moved: the initial access vector and each lateral technique | Email and click telemetry, logon types, remote-execution artifacts |\n\n### Set the time window deliberately\n\nAnchor on T0, the first confirmed malicious event you know about. Then search backwards at least 24 hours before T0 and forwards to now, and widen the backward window every time you find an earlier event. Two traps:\n\n- **Retention.** If advanced hunting keeps 30 days and the earliest event sits on day 29, you have not found the start; you have found the edge of your data. Say so in the notes and pull archived logs (Sentinel long-term retention or the SIEM archive) before claiming a start time.\n- **Ingestion delay.** Cloud audit records can arrive minutes after the event. A query that ends at \"now\" may miss the last few minutes of activity.\n\n### Scope identity and cloud, not only hosts\n\nFor each account in Who, ask the cloud questions too. Did it sign in from a new IP, ASN or user agent? Does it hold active sessions or refresh tokens that could be replayed? Were MFA methods added? Did it consent to an OAuth application or create mailbox rules? An on-prem-only scope misses exactly the footholds that survive host reimaging.\n\n### The scope statement\n\nKeep a running statement in the case, with a confidence word on each line:\n\n- Hosts: WS-ACC-117 (confirmed), SRV-FILE02 (confirmed), others (sweep pending)\n- Accounts: d.levi (confirmed), it.klein (confirmed), svc_backup (possible, was logged on to SRV-FILE02 during the dump)\n- Window: 2026-10-06 13:36 UTC to now (start probable, email is earliest found)\n- Vector: phishing link (probable, click confirmed, landing page not yet analysed)\n\n### Questions to ask at the end of every pivot round\n\nDid this round add a host, account or indicator? If yes, it goes back through validation and gets its own pivots. If two consecutive rounds add nothing and every sweep has run, scope is stable and you can plan containment.",
        "codeExample": "// Scope board: keep this at the top of the case and update it every round\n\nQUESTION  ANSWER SO FAR                                     CONFIDENCE  OPEN ACTION\nWho       d.levi, it.klein; svc_backup logged on during     confirmed / confirmed / possible\n          the dump on SRV-FILE02                                        check svc_backup use after 14:22\nWhat      LNK -> PowerShell beacon, scheduled-task          confirmed   check for data staging on SRV-FILE02\n          persistence, two LSASS dumps, PsExec movement\nWhen      2026-10-06 13:36 UTC -> now                       probable    confirm nothing earlier in archive\nWhere     WS-ACC-117, SRV-FILE02, d.levi mailbox            confirmed   IOC sweep of 203.0.113.77 estate-wide\nHow       phishing link; PsExec with stolen creds           probable    analyse landing page, check MFA logs\n\n// Round log\nRound 1: alert host -> logon source  (+1 host, +1 account)\nRound 2: source host -> process tree (+1 vector, +2 IOCs)\nRound 3: IOC sweep + cloud identity  (+1 mailbox rule, +1 MFA method)\nRound 4: sweeps re-run               (+0) -> scope stable"
